# Local dev/test-only key material, not used anywhere in production. High entropy is inherent
# to what these values are (a Fernet key, MinIO's single-master KMS key) - there is no "less
# secret-looking" valid value, and both are only ever read by the bundled local
# postgres/redis/minio containers spun up by docker-compose(-test).yaml for local dev and CI
# integration tests (see feat/s3-encryption's MinIO SSE fix and the encryption-at-rest work).
#
# ENCRYPTION_KEY (.env): the app's LocalEncryptionProvider Fernet key
# (src/connector/encryption/local_provider.py) - decrypts nothing outside this repo's own
# local/CI database, generated once with LocalEncryptionProvider.generate_key().
#
# MINIO_KMS_SECRET_KEY (docker-compose.yaml / docker-compose-test.yaml): MinIO's built-in
# static KMS key, required so MinIO accepts SSE-S3 (AES256) at all (it otherwise rejects
# PutBucketEncryption/PutObject with "KMS is not configured") - only ever protects the throwaway
# `minio_data` volume of the local/CI MinIO container.
#
# Same content, three fingerprints per introducing commit: gitleaks scans the *entire* repo
# history (all refs), not just the branch/PR under test, so every SHA that has ever introduced
# this content anywhere in the repo needs an entry here - it doesn't matter whether the branch
# being linted contains that commit or not. The commit itself also keeps getting rewritten
# (rebase/amend/squash) on whichever branch it lands on next, each time under a new SHA - append
# a fresh set here whenever gitleaks flags it again, same as the test-values.yaml fixture above.
# Currently covers: dev (9a088c9), feat/s3-encryption (8231bf4 -> 55d74c6 -> e4a5e33, rewritten
